  ☐ Extend scene-linear HDR rendering through the complete scene and postprocessing pipeline.
    - Audit render-target formats, copies, blending and shader outputs across opaque terrain,
      entities, transparency/OIT, liquids, sky, clouds and late held items. Preserve HDR radiance
      in floating-point targets through scene composition; changing primary format alone is insufficient.
    - Coordinate with the entity/late-path, atmosphere and liquid tasks above so all contributing
      passes use consistent linear-color and lighting units with LumOn enabled or disabled.
    - Move exposure and the display resolve after scene lighting and transparency composition.
      Adapt bloom, fog and grading to their intended color spaces; remove premature clamps and
      duplicate transfer functions. Keep lighting caches independent of display exposure.
    - Separate UI composition from scene exposure and preserve readable, stable UI brightness.
      Retain an explicit SDR output path; internal HDR must also benefit ordinary SDR displays.
    - Validate highlight preservation, blending, emission, exposure, UI, resize/reload and both
      lighting modes through focused tests and user-run visual acceptance. Measure bandwidth,
      memory and pass costs. Extend docs/PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md with the final boundaries.

  ☐ Investigate and implement native HDR monitor output with an SDR fallback.
    - First trace the game's OpenGL context, window framebuffer and presentation ownership.
      Establish whether VGE can configure HDR presentation directly or needs a platform-specific
      bridge; document supported platforms and feasibility before committing to that architecture.
    - Build on the scene-linear HDR pipeline above. Select an appropriate HDR presentation format
      and output color space, such as scRGB or HDR10 where supported; an HDR intermediate alone
      does not establish HDR display output. Preserve the existing SDR presentation path.
    - Detect display/OS HDR availability and handle enable/disable, monitor changes, window modes,
      resize and resource recreation without stale color-space state or unintended clipping.
    - Define scene-to-display luminance mapping, reference white, peak-brightness calibration,
      gamut mapping and UI brightness. Apply the output transfer exactly once and keep engine
      grading consistent with the selected output mode.
    - Validate capability/failure handling with focused tests, then obtain user-run acceptance on
      supported HDR displays and SDR fallback. Record presentation/interop costs and limitations;
      do not claim monitor-output support from floating-point framebuffer tests alone.
